{"chunk_id": "4bb605dfb4c7", "text": "King Dasaratha lies collapsed in humiliation and grief, compared to the fallen celestial king Yayati. Yet Kaikeyi, unmoved and intent on securing the promises he once granted her, presses him again to fulfill the two boons. She challenges his reputation for truthfulness, demanding he honor his vow. In anguish and anger, Dasaratha denounces her cruelty and laments the thought of sending Rama\u2014virtuous, beloved, and long-awaited after years without an heir\u2014into exile in the Dandaka forest. He imagines the shame of explaining such an act even before the gods and declares that death before witnessing Rama\u2019s suffering would have been kinder. As night falls, the king remains shattered, finding no comfort even in the moonlight. The passage heightens the moral crisis of the epic: the fatal collision between a king\u2019s vow and a father\u2019s love.\nKaikeyi presses her demanded boons while King Dasaratha, collapsed in anguish, laments the thought of exiling his beloved son Rama.\nThemes: parental grief, duty vs love, ruthlessness of Kaikeyi, truth and vows, impending exile", "vector": [0.00014745428052265197, -0.018242770805954933, -0.014078368432819843, 0.00010337542335037142, -0.009289304725825787, 0.023898297920823097, -0.033449556678533554, -0.00039062267751432955, -0.02311915159225464, -0.01610683463513851, 0.013997767120599747, 0.009819930419325829, 0.010847597382962704, 0.020298104733228683, -0.006511916872113943, 0.007086201570928097, 0.04935488849878311, -0.02143995836377144, 0.03766769543290138, -0.013561176136136055, 0.0073414393700659275, -0.005134977400302887, -0.0028445557691156864, 0.022783312946558, -0.02066081203520298, -0.0029167612083256245, 0.02689398266375065, -0.026249172165989876, 0.023602761328220367, -0.00846314150840044, -0.014024633914232254, -0.011700628325343132, 0.0046681612730026245, -0.010632661171257496, -0.02682681381702423, 0.0164426751434803, -0.0024784912820905447, -0.0007153369369916